Let $Q$ be the point on the unit circle that is $45^\circ$ counterclockwise from $(1,0)$, and let $E$ be the foot of the altitude from $Q$ to the $x$-axis.

[asy]

pair A,C,Q,O,E;

draw((0,-1.2)--(0,1.2),p=black+1.2bp,Arrows(0.15cm));

draw((-1.2,0)--(1.2,0),p=black+1.2bp,Arrows(0.15cm));

A = (1,0);

O= (0,0);

label("$x$",(1.2,0),SE);

label("$y$",(0,1.2),NE);

Q = rotate(45)*A;

E = foot(Q,A,-A);

draw(O--Q--E);

draw(rightanglemark(O,E,Q,2));

draw(Circle(O,1));

label("$O$",O,SE);

label("$Q$",Q,NE);

//label("$A$",A,SE);

label("$E$",E,S);

[/asy]

Triangle $QOE$ is a 45-45-90 triangle, implying both legs are equal. Since $OE$ is half the hypotenuse of the unit circle, $OE = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Therefore, the coordinates of $Q$ are $\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)$, so $\sin 45^\circ = \boxed{\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}}$.

